parts.text:
Now, `readFrameworkResource` uses classloader `getResourceAsStream`. For the extension's own loader, `getClass().getClassLoader()` can see its jar. Good.

However, there is a potential issue: `getResourceAsStream` may log a warning if the path starts with `/`? We're not prefixing with `/`, good.

Now, the `deep` method calls `frameworkFile` which now may read TCCL. Good.

Now update the Javadoc in `deep` maybe not necessary.

Also, the `MediaType.APPLICATION_OCTET_STREAM_TYPE` for `.js`? `mediaTypeOf` maps `.js` to `application/javascript`. Good.

Now Spring: no code change needed. But I said I'd update the Javadoc. Let's update the class constants/docs minimally.

Now tests. Let me look at the test structure to add new test files. Spring test uses KotlinFixtures and `PathlandApp`. Let me read `KotlinFixtures.kt` and an existing test to model. Also check how the manifest name can be obtained. I'll read `PathlandAssetsTest` for a helper. But `PathlandAssets` is package-private in `dev.pathland.server`; tests in `dev.pathland.spring` can't access. I'll read the manifest via classpath in the test.

Spring test plan:
```java
@SpringBootTest(classes = DomRendererBundleTest.TestApp.class)
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
class DomRendererBundleTest {
    @SpringBootApplication
    static class TestApp {
        @Bean PathlandApp pathlandApp() { return () -> KotlinFixtures.text("Hello"); }
    }

    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;

    private static String bundleName() throws Exception {
        try (InputStream in = Thread.currentThread().getContextClassLoader()
                .getResourceAsStream("static/_pathland/dom-renderer.current")) {
            assertNotNull(in, "the starter jar ships the DOM renderer manifest");
            String line = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(in, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)).readLine();
            return line.trim();
        }
    }

    @Test
    void bundleIsServedAtGlobalAndMountedBases() throws Exception {
        String name = bundleName();
        for (String path : new String[]{"/_pathland/" + name, "/app2/_pathland/" + name}) {
            mockMvc.perform(get(path))
                .andExpect(status().isOk())
                .andExpect(content().contentTypeCompatibleWith("application/javascript"))
                .andExpect(header().string("Cache-Control", containsString("immutable")))
                .andExpect(content().string(containsString("pathland"))); // non-empty? maybe not contains pathland
        }
    }

    @Test
    void ssrReferencesTheBundledScript() throws Exception {
        String name = bundleName();
        mockMvc.perform(get("/"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(content().string(containsString("/_pathland/" + name)))
            .andExpect(content().string(containsString("integrity=\"sha384-")));
    }
}
```

For body non-empty, use `.andExpect(content().string(not(emptyString())))` or check length. Simpler: use `MvcResult` and assert body length > 0. Or check it contains something from the bundle. The minified bundle likely contains `pathland` string. But safer: use `jsonPath`? no. I'll capture result and assert `getContentAsByteArray().length > 0`.

Need imports: `org.springframework.test.web.servlet.MvcResult`, `static ...status`, `header`, `content`, `get`, `containsString`, `assertTrue`.

Content type: Spring `MediaTypeFactory.getMediaType("dom-renderer-xxx.js")` returns `application/javascript`? Actually Spring's MediaTypeFactory may return `text/javascript` for `.js` depending on version. Let's use `content().contentTypeCompatibleWith(MediaType.valueOf("application/javascript"))`? If it returns `text/javascript`, that's not compatible with `application/javascript` (different types) — content type compatibility compares type/subtype. Hmm. In Spring Framework 6, `.js` maps to `text/javascript` per IANA? Let me think. `MediaTypeFactory` uses `MimeType` from `mime.types` maybe `.js -> text/javascript` in newer versions? Actually `MediaTypeFactory.getMediaType("foo.js")` returns `application/javascript` historically. In Spring 6.1, they might have changed to `text/javascript`. To be robust, assert the `Content-Type` header starts with either. Use `header().string("Content-Type", anyOf(containsString("javascript")))`. `header().string("Content-Type", containsString("javascript"))` works. Use Hamcrest `containsString`.

Quarkus test: `@QuarkusTest`. Need a mount? The global and mounted path both work; mounted `/app2/_pathland/...` is intercepted by `deep`. Read manifest via TCCL `META-INF/resources/_pathland/dom-renderer.current`. But in the Quarkus test module, test resources contain a stub `sw.js`; main resources contain the real bundle. TCCL should resolve the main resources. Good.

Quarkus HTTP client: use `@TestHTTPResource("/") URL base;` like `BaseCssTest`. Then GET `_pathland/<name>` and `app2/_pathland/<name>`. Assert 200, content-type contains javascript, cache-control immutable (for both? global is served by static handler which may not set immutable; mounted by `frameworkFile` sets immutable). So don't assert immutable on global, only mounted for Quarkus. Or assert body non-empty and status. Let's assert:
- global: 200, JS content type, non-empty body.
- mounted: 200, JS content type, immutable header, non-empty body.
- SSR references the bundle with integrity.

Wait: For global, will Quarkus static handler serve `/_pathland/dom-renderer-<hash>.js` from the extension jar in test mode? Yes. It might return content type `text/javascript` or `application/javascript`. Assert contains "javascript".

Also, will the Quarkus test's `/app2/_pathland/<name>` route hit `deep`? `deep` path regex `{path:(?!_pathland)(?!q/).*}` matches `app2/_pathland/dom-renderer-...`. Then `path.indexOf("/_pathland/")` = 4, substring → file, calls `frameworkFile`. Good.

But wait: there's now also `@Path("/_pathland/{file}")` global fallback route. For `/app2/_pathland/...`, that doesn't match; `deep` handles. Fine.

Potential routing conflict: `/app2/_pathland/dom-renderer-x.js` could also match `@Path("/_pathland/{name}.css")`? No.

Now, the SSR HTML: `PathlandSession.renderHtml` emits `<script src="/_pathland/dom-renderer-<hash>.js" integrity="sha384-...">`. In the Quarkus test, does `PathlandAssets` resolve the manifest from the extension main resources? Yes. Assert contains `"integrity=\"sha384-` and the name.

But note: the SSR test app in the Quarkus module may use a Rust renderer that needs the native lib. Existing tests handle `assumeTrue`. For the SSR assertion we might skip if renderer unavailable (like `BaseCssTest` does via cssName). Actually even without the Rust renderer, `renderHtml` returns an "unavailable" document with no script. So to assert the script, we need the renderer. Existing `BaseCssTest` uses `assumeTrue(renderer != null...)`. We can do the same for the SSR reference test, or skip that part. Let me follow the pattern: `assumeTrue(HtmlRenderer.tryInstance() != null, ...)`.

But the bundle-serving test doesn't depend on the renderer. Good.

Now, where does the manifest come from in the Quarkus test? The extension's main resources. But note: when running `mvn test` in `pathland-quarkus`, the test classpath includes `target/classes` (main resources with bundle) and `src/test/resources` (stub sw.js). TCCL getResourceAsStream("META-INF/resources/_pathland/dom-renderer.current") → target/classes. Good.

For the Spring test, `src/main/resources/static/_pathland/dom-renderer.current` → target/classes. Good.

Now, one concern: When running `mvn test` on the whole reactor, the Spring starter's `target/classes` will contain the bundle. But for the `pathland-spring-boot-starter` module, `mvn test` runs `process-resources` first, copying `src/main/resources` to `target/classes`. Good.

Now, let's write the tests. First read the Spring `KotlinFixtures.kt` to confirm `text`.

